# Cogeto: the instance environment file. Copy to .env to override.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# HOW TO READ THIS FILE
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Every entry carries a tag saying WHO OWNS IT. That is the distinction that
# matters: there are around a hundred and sixty variables here and an operator
# is responsible for about six of them.
#
#   [operator]   you decide this. The whole set is the FIRST BLOCK below, and
#                that block is short on purpose.
#   [installer]  `cogeto install` generates it per instance and writes it into
#                /srv/cogeto/.env. You never type it; you back it up.
#   [knob]       a default that is already right. Change one only when a
#                specific symptom says to, never as part of setting up.
#   [dev]        source checkout only. A deployed instance ignores it, or the
#                operator script manages it for you.
#
# Each entry also says whether it is REQUIRED (the stack refuses to start
# without it) or optional. Everything unmarked is optional and has a working
# default baked into the compose file, which is why a fresh clone runs with no
# .env at all. Those clone defaults are DEV SECRETS: a preflight container
# refuses every one of them on a non-localhost domain, so they cannot reach a
# real deployment by accident.
#
# .env is gitignored. Secrets are never committed.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WHAT IS NOT IN THIS FILE
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# MODEL AND PROVIDER CONFIGURATION. Providers, models and their API keys are
# records an administrator manages in the INTERFACE (Providers and Models in
# the left rail), stored in the instance database and encrypted under
# COGETO_MASTER_KEY below. The environment carries none of it: no seed, no
# fallback, no override. A model variable left in this file has no effect
# whatsoever. The ONE deliberate exception is the managed provider on a
# hosted plan (the COGETO_MANAGED_PROVIDER_* pair below): it reconciles
# exactly one locked provider row at boot, and every other provider and
# assignment stays interface-only. Reference: docs/features/models.md.
#
# CONNECTIONS TO EXTERNAL SYSTEMS (Confluence and anything after it), for the
# same reason: a connector is a record with credentials sealed in the
# database, added under Connections in the interface.
#
# ADDRESSES AND PATHS THE COMPOSE FILE FIXES. Roughly forty variables exist
# only inside docker-compose.yml, where one service's address or mount path is
# written into another's environment: COGETO_DATABASE_URL, COGETO_QDRANT_URL,
# COGETO_S3_URL, COGETO_S3_BUCKET, COGETO_HTTP_PORT, COGETO_INTAKE_URL,
# COGETO_WEB_CONFIG_FILE, COGETO_INSTANCE_PUBKEY_DIR, COGETO_COMPOSE_PROFILES,
# COGETO_ASSERT_NO_PRIVATE_KEY, COGETO_ISSUER with COGETO_REDIRECT_URI,
# COGETO_POST_LOGOUT_URI, COGETO_OIDC_ISSUER, COGETO_OIDC_INTERNAL_URL and
# COGETO_OIDC_EXTERNAL_DOMAIN beside it, ZITADEL_INTERNAL_URL,
# ZITADEL_EXTERNAL_DOMAIN, ZITADEL_PAT_FILE. They are derived from the values
# below, they are not knobs, and setting them in .env is a way to break the
# stack rather than to configure it.
#
# DEVELOPER AND CI TOOLING. Read only by tools, never by a running instance:
# COGETO_EVAL_GATE and COGETO_EVAL_CACHE (the eval jobs), COGETO_SEED_ORG and
# COGETO_SEED_OWNER (the dev seed), COGETO_DEMO_DIR (the demo corpus in tests),
# COGETO_MAIL_HOST and COGETO_MAIL_PORT (scripts/dev/send-test-email.mjs).
# Two more belong to the operator script and are passed on ITS command line
# rather than set in .env: COGETO_ROOT (the instance directory, also --root)
# and COGETO_SKIP_RESOURCE_CHECK (the preflight escape hatch).
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 1. THE SET AN OPERATOR ACTUALLY TOUCHES
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── External identity of the instance ── [operator] REQUIRED
# The domain the browser uses, and Zitadel's external domain (routing is
# Host-based). `cogeto install --domain` asks for it and writes it; changing it
# later is `cogeto configure --domain`, never a hand edit, because the identity
# provider has to be re-provisioned with it.
COGETO_EXTERNAL_DOMAIN=localhost

# ── The rest of what the installer asks you ── [operator] REQUIRED on a
# deployed instance; absent from a source checkout, which serves no public TLS.
# Written by `cogeto install`, listed here so nothing in /srv/cogeto/.env is
# unexplained:
#  - COGETO_ACME_EMAIL: where Let's Encrypt sends certificate expiry notices.
#    `cogeto install --acme-email` asks for it.
#  - COGETO_VERSION: the release the instance runs. `cogeto install` and
#    `cogeto upgrade` maintain it, and every image tag in the compose file
#    resolves through it. Editing it by hand moves the stack to an unverified
#    version; use `cogeto upgrade`.

# ── Optional capabilities ── [operator, all optional]
# Do not hand-edit these: `sudo cogeto features enable|disable <id>` maintains
# them, because a capability is a compose profile plus its flags plus, for
# some, a firewall rule and a pulled image. The five switchable capabilities
# are redaction, research, mail, demo and consoles; their own knobs are in
# section 3.
#  - COMPOSE_PROFILES: the comma-separated profile list, the ONE signal a
#    container has for which profiles are active (a --profile flag on the
#    command line is invisible from inside).
#  - COGETO_RESEARCH_ENABLED / COGETO_CONSOLES_ENABLED / COGETO_MAIL_ENABLED:
#    explicit enablement for one-off `docker compose --profile ...` dev runs.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=
#COGETO_RESEARCH_ENABLED=
#COGETO_CONSOLES_ENABLED=
#COGETO_MAIL_ENABLED=

# ── Application behaviour ── [operator, all optional]
# pino level for app and worker: trace|debug|info|warn|error|fatal. Default info.
COGETO_LOG_LEVEL=info
# Instance display timezone: relative dates ("today"/"tomorrow") resolve
# against local midnight in THIS IANA zone, not UTC. Default Europe/Zagreb.
#COGETO_TIMEZONE=Europe/Zagreb
# Zitadel project role that gates the operator System view. Default admin.
# Must match what the identity bootstrap grants (section 2).
#COGETO_ADMIN_ROLE=admin
# Nightly-job overdue threshold for the capability registry, in hours
# (default 26: one nightly slot plus slack). Reported in System and health.
#COGETO_JOBS_OVERDUE_HOURS=26

# ── Model runtime knobs ── [knob, all optional]
# The only model-adjacent environment configuration there is, kept here
# because each is a deployment fact rather than a model choice. WHICH models
# run is configured in the interface.
#
# Per-tier request timeouts for SELF-HOSTED endpoints (ms). Local inference
# answers in seconds to minutes; hosted providers keep no timeout at all.
# Defaults 300000 / 300000 / 120000 / 600000. These are the only names for
# this setting: the Ollama-era COGETO_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_*_MS alias was retired in
# issue #567 and is inert.
#COGETO_MODEL_TIMEOUT_PIPELINE_MS=
#COGETO_MODEL_TIMEOUT_ANSWER_MS=
#COGETO_MODEL_TIMEOUT_EMBEDDINGS_MS=
#COGETO_MODEL_TIMEOUT_VISION_MS=
# maxTokens multiplier applied once a binding is PROBED as a reasoning model
# (some models return their thinking in a separate field, detected at boot by
# sending a trivial prompt; a cap sized for an answer is not sized for an
# answer plus its deliberation). Only applied to bindings that actually
# reasoned. Default 4. Documented once, here.
#COGETO_REASONING_HEADROOM=4
# How long the reasoning and vision PROBES may take (ms). Generous by
# default: a cold model warms slowly, and declaring a working runtime dead is
# the worse error. Defaults 30000 each.
#COGETO_REASONING_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
#COGETO_VISION_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 2. SECRETS AND IDENTITY: every entry below is generated by the installer
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Nothing in this section is yours to invent. `cogeto install` generates the
# whole set into /srv/cogeto/.env (mode 600); your job is to put that file in
# your vault, and to put it back if the instance is ever rebuilt. Rotate one
# with `cogeto configure --regenerate NAME`, which refuses the data-bound ones
# by name rather than letting you destroy data with a typo.

# ── The instance master key ── [installer] REQUIRED before a provider key is
# stored; optional until then, and the failure names this variable.
# Encrypts the provider API keys entered in the interface; it stays HERE and
# never goes into the database, because a key that guards a database cannot
# live inside it. Generate one by hand with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# DATA-BOUND: it never changes after that. Rotating it makes every stored
# provider key unreadable, and there is no recovery except re-entering them.
COGETO_MASTER_KEY=

# ── The managed provider (hosted plans only) ── [platform] OPTIONAL
# On a hosting platform, exactly ONE provider row per instance may be
# MANAGED: reconciled at boot from a platform-rendered configuration file
# plus this bootstrap key, then locked in the interface (no edit, no key
# field, no delete). The file's path (inside the container: the compose
# mounts ./managed-provider read-only) names the label, the endpoint, the
# served model names and the initial assignments; the key lives HERE and in
# neither file. Both set: reconcile. One without the other: the boot refuses,
# naming what is missing. Neither set (every self-managed instance): no
# managed provider, and the interface stays the only place models are
# configured. Rotate the key by re-rendering this file and restarting.
# Contract: project/infra/managed-provider.example.json and
# docs/features/models.md.
# COGETO_MANAGED_PROVIDER_FILE=/managed-provider/managed-provider.json
# COGETO_MANAGED_PROVIDER_API_KEY=

# ── PostgreSQL (source of truth) ── [installer] REQUIRED
# Superuser password. Since wave 3 of the audit 2.0 remediation (SEC-1) the
# superuser is BREAK-GLASS ONLY: no long-running service connects with it.
# The db-init one-shot uses it to provision the three least-privilege
# identities below (idempotent; passwords re-sync from .env on every
# `compose up`, which is what makes the three rotatable).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=cogeto-dev-password
# The runtime role for app + worker: table-level DML only, no DDL.
COGETO_APP_DB_PASSWORD=cogeto-dev-app-db-password
# The schema owner; only the migrate entrypoint connects with it.
COGETO_MIGRATE_DB_PASSWORD=cogeto-dev-migrate-db-password
# Zitadel's bootstrap/migration DB admin (CREATEDB/CREATEROLE, not superuser).
ZITADEL_DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=zitadel-dev-admin-password

# ── MinIO (file bytes, S3 API, SSE-S3 encryption at rest) ── [installer]
# Root credential: provisioning only (minio-init and the dev console). REQUIRED.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=cogeto
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=cogeto-dev-password
# Scoped application credential, provisioned by minio-init: bucket-only
# access, no admin API. The secret is REQUIRED; the access key defaults to
# cogeto-app. Rotatable (minio-init re-syncs on `compose up`).
COGETO_S3_ACCESS_KEY=cogeto-app
COGETO_S3_SECRET_KEY=cogeto-dev-app-password
# Server-side encryption master key (single-node KMS). REQUIRED. Format is
# <key-name>:<base64 of 32 random bytes>. DATA-BOUND: losing it makes stored
# objects unreadable; back it up with the instance secrets.
MINIO_KMS_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Qdrant (vector index) ── [installer] REQUIRED on a deployed instance
# API key, passed to the qdrant service as QDRANT__SERVICE__API_KEY. The dev
# stack keeps Qdrant on the internal network with no key.
#COGETO_QDRANT_API_KEY=

# ── Zitadel (identity, SSO) ── [installer] REQUIRED
# Symmetric masterkey for Zitadel's encryption keys; MUST be exactly 32 chars.
ZITADEL_MASTERKEY=MasterkeyNeedsToHave32Characters
# Password for the dedicated zitadel database user (created on first init).
ZITADEL_DB_PASSWORD=zitadel-dev-password
# First organization and human admin created on first init.
ZITADEL_ORG_NAME=Cogeto
ZITADEL_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin@cogeto.localhost
# Must satisfy Zitadel's default policy: upper + lower + digit + symbol.
ZITADEL_ADMIN_PASSWORD=DevPassword1!
# Expiry of the one-shot bootstrap PAT (SEC-16). zitadel-init REVOKES the PAT
# as soon as provisioning succeeds, so this is the backstop, not the control.
# The installer generates install date + 14 days per instance.
#ZITADEL_BOOTSTRAP_PAT_EXPIRY=2030-01-01T00:00:00Z

# ── Identity bootstrap: the machine user and its state file ── [knob]
# The one-shot that provisions the OIDC application runs as a machine user and
# records "provisioning already succeeded" in a state file, so later runs
# short-circuit instead of needing a live PAT. Both default correctly and are
# here because their VALUES are operational facts, not because you should
# change them: the runbook's domain-change procedure deletes the state file
# and writes a fresh token beside it, and both paths are inside the
# zitadel-machinekey volume, not on the host filesystem.
#  - ZITADEL_BOOTSTRAP_MACHINE_USERNAME: default cogeto-bootstrap, which is the
#    user you open in the Zitadel console to mint a replacement token.
#  - ZITADEL_BOOTSTRAP_STATE_FILE: default /machinekey/bootstrap-state.json,
#    beside the PAT at /machinekey/pat.txt.
#ZITADEL_BOOTSTRAP_MACHINE_USERNAME=cogeto-bootstrap
#ZITADEL_BOOTSTRAP_STATE_FILE=/machinekey/bootstrap-state.json

# ── Identity notification mail (outbound SMTP for Zitadel) ── [knob] OPTIONAL
# The relay Zitadel sends invitations, address verification and password
# resets through. Leave EVERY value empty (the default) and the instance runs
# with no outbound mail: nothing is configured, and an invitation sent from
# the Zitadel console silently never arrives, so hand initial passwords over
# out of band (operator runbook section 4).
#
# Set them and zitadel-init applies them through Zitadel's API while it
# provisions, on every provisioning run: rotating the credential is a
# re-rendered .env plus `docker compose up`. Two rules worth knowing before
# you fill them in:
#   - a PARTIAL set is REFUSED by name, never half applied. Required together:
#     HOST, FROM, FROM_NAME and TLS; USER and PASSWORD are optional but must
#     be given together (a relay needing no credentials is legitimate, half a
#     credential is not).
#   - TLS must be spelled exactly true or false. There is no default: whether
#     the credential travels encrypted is not a silent decision.
# Changing any of these on an instance that is ALREADY provisioned needs the
# runbook's re-provisioning recovery, because the bootstrap credential is
# destroyed once provisioning succeeds (SEC-16). The init says so and lets the
# instance come up rather than taking it down over mail. Removing them again
# stops Cogeto managing the relay but does NOT switch off one Zitadel already
# has: deactivate that in the Zitadel console.
#ZITADEL_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com:587
#ZITADEL_SMTP_USER=
#ZITADEL_SMTP_PASSWORD=
#ZITADEL_SMTP_TLS=true
#ZITADEL_SMTP_FROM=no-reply@example.com
#ZITADEL_SMTP_FROM_NAME=Cogeto

# ── Inbound email intake secret ── [installer] REQUIRED with email capture
# Shared secret the Haraka container presents on POST /api/email/intake. The
# deploy stack REQUIRES it; refused as a known dev value on a non-localhost
# deployment (secret-preflight).
#COGETO_MAIL_INTAKE_TOKEN=

# ── Web research secret ── [installer, research profile only]
# SearXNG server secret; `cogeto features enable research` generates it.
#SEARXNG_SECRET=

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 3. THE OPTIONAL CAPABILITIES' OWN KNOBS
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Turn a capability on or off with `cogeto features` (section 1). What follows
# is what each one exposes once it is on. All optional.

# ── Inbound email (profile mail; SEC-14 opt-in) ── [knob]
# Sender-routed capture: mail from a registered user's own address is captured
# for that user; other senders are routed by each user's allowlist. The
# bootstrap admin account never captures; compose derives its address from
# ZITADEL_ADMIN_USERNAME. Override only if the admin login differs.
#COGETO_ADMIN_USER_EMAIL=admin@cogeto.localhost
# The instance's unique inbound address; the installer derives
# capture@in.<domain> and the UI shows it with forwarding-setup guidance.
#COGETO_MAIL_INBOUND_ADDRESS=capture@in.localhost
# Max bytes for a whole inbound message / a single attachment (default 25 MiB
# each).
#COGETO_MAIL_MAX_BYTES=26214400
#COGETO_MAIL_ATTACHMENTS_MAX_BYTES=26214400
# Host port the SMTP listener publishes (default 25) and the in-network SMTP
# address of the mail container (for the health probe).
#COGETO_MAIL_HOST_PORT=25
#COGETO_MAIL_SMTP_ADDRESS=mail:2525
# Sender authentication (default on): capture-for-claimed-user only when the
# SMTP sender passed SPF, so a spoofed From cannot inject that user's memory.
#COGETO_MAIL_REQUIRE_SPF=1
# Per-sender accepted-message cap within the intake window; 0 disables.
#COGETO_MAIL_INTAKE_MAX_PER_SENDER=60
#COGETO_MAIL_INTAKE_RATE_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# Inbound STARTTLS: paths (inside the mail container's mail-tls volume) to the
# instance cert/key, and the hostname the edge obtains that certificate for.
# On a deployed instance ALL THREE are automatic and need no operator action
# beyond one DNS record: `cogeto features enable mail` sets the site, the edge
# obtains and renews the certificate, and a sidecar copies it in. Touch these
# only for an operator-supplied certificate (your own CA or a wildcard), which
# is a documented override with exact ownership requirements. One description
# of the whole mechanism, and the override:
# docs/operations/email-inbound.md.
#COGETO_MAIL_TLS_CERT=/app/tls/cert.pem
#COGETO_MAIL_TLS_KEY=/app/tls/key.pem
#COGETO_MAIL_TLS_SITE=
# WHO OWNS THAT CERTIFICATE: `automatic` (the default: the edge obtains and
# renews it, the sidecar installs it) or `operator` (you supply cert.pem and
# key.pem yourself; nothing is ordered, nothing is copied, your files are never
# overwritten, and renewal is yours). Set it with the operator script, which is
# what makes the override survive an upgrade:
#   sudo cogeto configure --mail-tls-mode operator
# Editing this line by hand is not enough on its own; the command also blanks
# COGETO_MAIL_TLS_SITE, which is what keeps the edge from ordering. Going back
# is `--mail-tls-mode automatic`, which asks you to confirm, because the edge
# then overwrites your material with its own.
#COGETO_MAIL_TLS_MODE=automatic

# ── Web research (profile research) ── [knob]
# Discovery is the self-hosted SearXNG container; the narrow fetcher pulls
# the selected pages server-side.
#  - COGETO_SEARXNG_URL: SearXNG base URL on the internal network (compose
#    defaults to the service name). Unset or unreachable: research answers a
#    typed "search unavailable"; nothing else is affected.
#COGETO_SEARXNG_URL=http://searxng:8080
#COGETO_RESEARCH_RESULT_CAP=8
#COGETO_RESEARCH_SEARCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
#COGETO_RESEARCH_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
#COGETO_RESEARCH_FETCH_MAX_BYTES=5242880
#COGETO_RESEARCH_PAGES_PER_RUN=5
# 1 = additionally retain the sanitised raw HTML as a scoped, encrypted MinIO
# object (default off: clean text + URL are the record).
#COGETO_RESEARCH_RETAIN_HTML=
# Per-user daily research budgets.
#COGETO_DAILY_RESEARCH_SEARCHES=40
#COGETO_DAILY_RESEARCH_PAGES=100
# Named skills: max approved queries in one run's plan, and pages read per
# query (clamped to the per-run page cap).
#COGETO_SKILL_MAX_QUERIES=6
#COGETO_SKILL_PAGES_PER_QUERY=3

# ── Redaction mode (spec §12.2, profile redaction) ── [knob]
# Local CPU NER pseudonymizes sensitive entities before any external model
# call and re-identifies the response. If enabled and the sidecar is
# unreachable, model calls FAIL CLOSED.
#
# Do NOT set these by hand on a customer instance: run
# `sudo cogeto features enable redaction`, which sets all three, adds the
# profile, pulls and cosign-verifies the image, and prints what to decide
# first. It costs roughly 0.7 to 1 GB RSS and measurably weakens
# entity-anchored retrieval, so it is an INSTANCE-LIFETIME choice: switching
# later means a reindex. Availability, cost and the honest limits are stated
# once, in docs/security/data-sovereignty-and-redaction.md.
#  - REDACTION_ENABLED: turn the gateway's redaction stage on (1/true).
#  - REDACTION_URL: sidecar base URL (compose defaults to the service name).
#  - REDACTION_SPACY_MODEL: NER model, passed to the sidecar as SPACY_MODEL
#    (en_core_web_lg default).
#  - REDACTION_REQUIRED: fail-closed assertion; set 1 to REFUSE boot unless
#    redaction is actually enabled.
#REDACTION_ENABLED=
#REDACTION_URL=http://redaction:8080
#REDACTION_SPACY_MODEL=en_core_web_lg
#REDACTION_REQUIRED=

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 4. LIMITS AND CAPS: defaults to leave alone
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Every one of these is [knob] and optional, with a sane default in code.
# Nothing here needs a decision at install time. Raise or lower one when a
# specific instance shows a specific symptom, and write down why.

# ── Abuse / DoS limits ──
# demoMode auto-tightens the aggressive set. A rate-limit bucket of 0 is
# unlimited. Window is shared by all buckets.
#COGETO_RATELIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
#COGETO_RATELIMIT_CHAT=30
#COGETO_RATELIMIT_CAPTURE=60
#COGETO_RATELIMIT_REMEMBER=30
#COGETO_RATELIMIT_UPLOAD=20
# Per-user daily model budget, enforced in the gateway on user-attributed
# calls, app and worker sharing one durable total (SEC-18/SEC-10). Estimated
# tokens ~= chars/4. On exhaustion: 429 until UTC midnight; worker jobs retry
# with backoff.
#COGETO_MODEL_DAILY_CALLS=10000
#COGETO_MODEL_DAILY_TOKENS=20000000
# Per-user daily capture/upload caps (bound the pipeline work one user can
# drive).
#COGETO_DAILY_CAPTURE_MAX=1000
#COGETO_DAILY_UPLOAD_MAX=300
# Chat SSE bounds: concurrent streams per principal, idle + max duration.
#COGETO_SSE_MAX_CONCURRENT=3
#COGETO_SSE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
#COGETO_SSE_MAX_DURATION_SECONDS=180

# ── Document parsing and reading ──
# Decompressed-text length, chunk count, per-parse wall-clock timeout, and
# max facts admitted per source.
#COGETO_PARSE_MAX_TEXT_CHARS=1000000
#COGETO_PARSE_MAX_CHUNKS=200
#COGETO_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
#COGETO_EXTRACT_MAX_FACTS=100
# Spreadsheets: how many data rows become statements, per sheet and per file.
# A read stopped by either cap is recorded on the source and shown in the
# drawer, so a partly read file never looks whole.
#COGETO_PARSE_MAX_SHEET_ROWS=5000
#COGETO_PARSE_MAX_FILE_ROWS=20000
# How CSV bytes are decoded when they are not valid UTF-8 and carry no BOM;
# windows-1250 carries the Croatian letters.
#COGETO_PARSE_CSV_FALLBACK_ENCODING=windows-1250
# Vision page caps: how many pages one document, and one user per day, may
# escalate to the vision tier (the vision model itself is assigned in the
# interface; unassigned is a complete answer and the ladder stops at OCR).
#COGETO_VISION_PAGES_PER_DOCUMENT=20
#COGETO_VISION_PAGES_PER_USER_DAILY=100
# Bulk import: how many import documents may be queued or processing at once
# (1..8). Default 1 keeps a worker slot free for interactive work.
#COGETO_IMPORT_IN_FLIGHT=1

# ── File upload and downloads ──
# Max bytes per upload (default 25 MiB).
#COGETO_UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=26214400
# Presigned download-URL lifetime in seconds (default 300; short-lived).
#COGETO_DOWNLOAD_URL_TTL_SECONDS=300
# Browser-reachable object-storage origin used ONLY to sign presigned
# download URLs. Compose defaults to https://s3.localhost for dev; the
# installer derives https://s3.<domain>.
#COGETO_S3_PUBLIC_URL=https://s3.localhost

# ── Postgres pool ──
# Connection-pool ceiling PER PROCESS, sized against worker concurrency (2);
# the pipeline holds its idempotency tx open across model calls. Default 10.
#COGETO_PG_POOL_MAX=10

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 5. DEV CHECKOUT AND PATHS: a deployed instance never touches these
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Paths the processes read at boot ── [dev]
# Compose sets sensible values; override only for unusual layouts (e.g.
# running entrypoints outside Docker).
#  - COGETO_MIGRATIONS_DIR: reviewable SQL files for the migrate entrypoint.
#  - COGETO_PROMPTS_DIR: versioned prompt artifacts (default project/prompts).
#  - COGETO_INSTANCE_KEY_DIR: instance ed25519 signing keypair (deletion
#    receipts, spec §11.1); compose mounts the instance-keys volume here.
#COGETO_MIGRATIONS_DIR=
#COGETO_PROMPTS_DIR=
#COGETO_INSTANCE_KEY_DIR=
# Findings report inputs: vendored fonts, canonical brand assets, and the
# published trust-score artifacts. Defaults are the repo-relative paths the
# Docker image reproduces; a missing trust-scores directory is honest (the
# report states no published measurement exists).
#COGETO_REPORT_FONTS_DIR=project/fonts
#COGETO_REPORT_BRAND_DIR=assets/brand
#COGETO_TRUST_SCORES_DIR=eval/trust-scores
# MinIO console origin behind the Caddy edge (dev consoles profile). Do NOT
# set MINIO_SERVER_URL (breaks console login).
#MINIO_BROWSER_REDIRECT_URL=https://minio.localhost

# ── Ana sandbox (profile demo; NEVER on a customer instance) ── [dev]
# The demo publishes a working access token for anyone, safe ONLY because the
# instance holds no real data. The deploy compose carries none of these, and a
# test asserts their absence rather than trusting the comment.
#  - COGETO_DEMO_MODE: force sandbox mode + the worker's scheduled reset.
#  - COGETO_PRODUCTION / COGETO_ENV=production: hard-refuse the demo
#    seed/reset.
#COGETO_DEMO_MODE=
#COGETO_PRODUCTION=
#COGETO_ENV=
#COGETO_DEMO_RESET_CRON=0 */6 * * *
#COGETO_DEMO_SESSION_FILE=/demo-config/session.json
# Demo-tightened limit overrides (public sandbox only).
#COGETO_DEMO_RATELIMIT_CHAT=12
#COGETO_DEMO_RATELIMIT_CAPTURE=60
#COGETO_DEMO_RATELIMIT_REMEMBER=15
#COGETO_DEMO_RATELIMIT_UPLOAD=10
#COGETO_DEMO_MODEL_DAILY_CALLS=2000
#COGETO_DEMO_MODEL_DAILY_TOKENS=4000000
#COGETO_DEMO_DAILY_CAPTURE_MAX=500
#COGETO_DEMO_DAILY_UPLOAD_MAX=100
#COGETO_DEMO_SSE_MAX_CONCURRENT=2
#COGETO_DEMO_DAILY_RESEARCH_SEARCHES=10
#COGETO_DEMO_DAILY_RESEARCH_PAGES=20

# ── Eval harness only (never read by a running instance) ── [dev]
# The harness resolves its model configuration from the environment on
# purpose: it runs in CI against no instance database, and pinning the
# configuration it measures is the point. Its full variable surface (the
# per-tier provider and model names, and the per-provider key and base-URL
# names) is documented in docs/eval-golden-set.md and is confined to the
# harness by
# model-config-env.spec.ts, which is why none of it appears above: on a
# running instance those names do nothing at all.
#COGETO_PROVIDER_GRADER=
#COGETO_MODEL_GRADER=
